PureDarwin
==========

Copyright (c) 2007-Present The PureDarwin Project and its contributors.

PureDarwin is a buildable, bootable operating system built on Apple's open
source Darwin releases. The PureDarwin Project fills the gaps those releases
leave with its own code: drivers, kernel extensions, frameworks, the desktop,
the Nix build and the system images. This file says what in this tree is
PureDarwin's own work, what comes from other projects, and how a fork or
derived work should credit it.

The licence of any single file is the one stated in that file (its SPDX line
and copyright notice). Nothing in this file changes the licence of any file.


1. PureDarwin's own work
------------------------

Files carrying "Copyright (c) <year> The PureDarwin Project" (or "PureDarwin
Project") were written for PureDarwin. As a rule, new PureDarwin code is under
"MPL-2.0 OR BSD-2-Clause", and code added inside a component with its own
licence takes that component's licence. This is a description of the project's
practice, not a grant: the SPDX line and notice in each file are what decide
its licence, and where they differ from this paragraph, the file wins. The
project's BSD-style licence text is PUREDARWIN_LICENSE.txt.

This includes, among others:

- The kernel: PureDarwin's XNU build and ports (xnu-12377 and earlier), the
  kernel collection tooling, the in-kernel FreeBSD/Linux compatibility layer
  (fbsdkpi) and the glue that runs drivers on it.
- Drivers and kernel extensions with the PD prefix (PDArmPlatformExpert,
  PDIOSurface, PDGOP, PDAmdGPU, PDNVLoader, PDNVUVM, PDDRM*, PDACPIPlatform and
  the rest), the ext4 driver, and the Ravyn* storage and USB drivers described
  in section 3.
- The frameworks PureDarwin implements or completes: AppKit, Foundation,
  CoreFoundation, CoreGraphics, CoreText, QuartzCore, OpenGL/CGL, Metal and
  the others under src/Frameworks, as far as their files carry PureDarwin's
  notice (see section 2 for Cocotron, ravynOS and NeoDarwin).
- The desktop: the Argon session, the Wayland compositor work on neuswc and
  neuwld, and the applications under src/Userspace.
- Running unmodified Mach-O applications built for macOS (for example the
  stock macOS build of Firefox, first running in October 2026) on these
  frameworks, and running Linux binaries through elftrans.
- The Nix build (flake.nix, nix/) and every image target it produces.

PureDarwin's bundle identifiers (org.puredarwin.*), its PD/pd naming, its boot
arguments and its install paths identify this project's work. A fork may of
course rename them, but renamed PureDarwin code is still PureDarwin's work and
should be credited as such (section 4).


2. Work from other projects
---------------------------

Each of these keeps its own copyright and licence notices, which must not be
removed.

- Apple Inc., NeXT Computer and Apple's open source releases: XNU, the IOKit
  families, libSystem, dyld, objc4 and the other Darwin components.
  Apple Public Source License 2.0 and the licences stated in each file
  (APPLE_LICENSE.txt, APPLE_DRIVER_LICENSE.txt).
  Apple publishes these releases at https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions,
  one repository per component, with a tag for each release.
  Their sources are copied into this tree rather than pulled in as submodules,
  so that one tree builds the whole system, and PureDarwin's changes are made
  in place. The few components the Nix build fetches instead are pinned to an
  exact release in their package under nix/. Section 2a lists where each
  component in this tree came from.
- NeoDarwin: dyld, through its dyld4linux, and corecrypto, which PureDarwin
  carries as an MPL-2.0 fork in external/corecrypto. PureDarwin's Foundation
  started from NeoDarwin's Foundation (Samuel Zormeister, MPL-2.0), a small
  first version with NSObject, NSZone, the CoreFoundation start-up hook and
  a few headers. Its notice stays on the files that came from it.
- Darling (the Darling developers): Darling Metal, the Metal frontend, fetched
  by the Nix build at a pinned commit and patched by
  nix/pkgs/gpu/darling-metal-pd.patch. MPL-2.0, per file. Indium (Ella
  Stanforth and the Darling developers): its interface headers and tests,
  also fetched. 0BSD. airjitd and indium-pd, which put that interface on
  the GPU, are PureDarwin's own clean-room work (section 1).
- NextBSD (iXsystems): libxpc under src/Libraries/XPC. BSD-2-Clause.
- OpenSSL: libcrypto and libssl, built by the Nix build and not stored in this
  tree. Security.framework links them privately, for TLS, CMS and PKCS#12, and
  the corecrypto fork's elliptic-curve operations use libcrypto. Apache-2.0.
- xxHash (Yann Collet): in src/Tools/airjitd/vulkan/vk.c. BSD-2-Clause.
- uACPI (Daniil Tatianin): the ACPI implementation PDACPIPlatform is built on,
  under src/Kernel/Extensions/PDACPIPlatform/uacpi. MIT.
- Cocotron (Christopher J. W. Lloyd, Dan Knapp, Johannes Fortmann and
  contributors): the original base of AppKit, CoreGraphics, Onyx2D, CoreText
  and CoreData, which came into this tree through ravynOS. MIT.
- FreeBSD: the LinuxKPI layer and other kernel code used by fbsdkpi, and libnv
  under src/Libraries/XPC. BSD-2-Clause and BSD-3-Clause.
- Haiku: the FreeBSD network compatibility code and headers used by fbsdkpi.
  MIT.
- ravynOS (Zoe Knox and contributors): the copies of the Cocotron frameworks
  above, QuartzCore, RavynAHCIPort and the code RavynXHCIPort started from
  (section 3). MIT.
- LLVM: the compiler, libc++, libc++abi, libunwind, compiler-rt and parts of
  LLVM's libc. Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception.
- Apple's build tools: cctools and ld64 under tools/cctools, and MIG under
  tools/mig. APSL-2.0.
- libtapi: Apple's release as packaged by Thomas Poechtrager's apple-libtapi,
  fetched by the Nix build. NCSA.
- Swift (swiftlang/swift): the runtime and standard library, built from
  source, and swift.org's prebuilt compiler, used only on the build machine.
  Apache-2.0 WITH Swift-exception. PureDarwin's layers under
  src/Libraries/Swift* are its own work (section 1).
- unifdef (Tony Finch) and xar (Rob Braun) under tools/. BSD licences.
- Wayland, wayland-protocols, wlroots, sway, neuswc and neuwld under
  src/ThirdParty: their own licences, as stated in each project.
- Mesa, drm-kmod and NVIDIA's driver package are fetched by the Nix build and
  are not stored in this tree. NVIDIA's closed resource manager is loaded
  unmodified by PDNVLoader and is never redistributed in modified form.

Some kernel files are dual-licensed by their authors (for example
"GPL-2.0 OR BSD-3-Clause" or "GPL-2.0-only OR MIT"). PureDarwin uses them under
the permissive option. GPL-only driver code (the Linux-derived DRM drivers in
drm-kmod) is not part of PureDarwin's own code and is not stored in this
tree: it is fetched at build time and built as optional kernel extensions.
That separation is how the project keeps GPL code out of its own sources. It
is not a statement that distributing those extensions, or a kernel collection
or image that includes them, is compatible with the GPL or with the licences of
the other components. Anyone distributing such builds has to assess that for
themselves.


